# WIP: pkgs/illumos/starter.nix rewrite for blocker 6 (fetchClosure)
#
# DO NOT APPLY until the 8 missing closures are published (see
# docs/... blocker-6 writeup). Applying now would trade a clear pure-eval
# error for a confusing 404 fetch failure across EVERY configuration that
# imports base.nix. The rewrite lands WITH the publish, not before.
#
# The change is ONE hunk: the `entry` binding's `value =` line. Everything
# else (filtering: published + deriver_resolves=y + system match; last-wins;
# row-shape validation) stays byte-for-byte identical, so base.nix and every
# config keep the same package selection.
#
# ---- BEFORE (current repo, pkgs/illumos/starter.nix:68-72) ----
#
#   # key by the tsv's own pname column (col 0) — no parseDrvName guessing needed.
#   entry = cols: {
#     name = builtins.elemAt cols 0;
#     value = builtins.storePath (builtins.elemAt cols 3);
#   };
#
# ---- AFTER ----
#
#   # key by the tsv's own pname column (col 0) — no parseDrvName guessing needed.
#   #
#   # value is fetchClosure, not storePath: builtins.storePath is FORBIDDEN in
#   # pure eval (nix eval .#), which blocks every toplevel from evaluating on a
#   # flake-native host. fetchClosure is the pure-legal equivalent — it names the
#   # substituter + the path, and Nix pulls the closure at eval time.
#   #
#   # inputAddressed = true is REQUIRED: our x86_64-solaris paths are
#   # INPUT-addressed, and plain fetchClosure errors "fromPath ... is not
#   # content-addressed" for that class. With inputAddressed = true, fetchClosure
#   # does NOT re-verify content-addressing, so SIGNATURE TRUST does all the
#   # integrity work: the path (and its whole closure) must be signed by a key in
#   # trusted-public-keys (cache.solnix.io-1:5HAV..., baked into base.nix). A path
#   # signed by an untrusted key is REFUSED at eval — which is why publishing only
#   # under 5HAV... is load-bearing, not incidental.
#   #
#   # Needs the fetch-closure experimental feature on the evaluating Nix. Resolves
#   # ONLY where the cache is trusted and the path + full closure are substitutable;
#   # on a daemon-client Nix that cannot substitute foreign-system paths (e.g. a
#   # Linux dev box) it fails at the daemon, which is expected and not a defect of
#   # this expression.
#   entry = cols: {
#     name = builtins.elemAt cols 0;
#     value = builtins.fetchClosure {
#       fromStore = "https://cache.solnix.io";
#       fromPath = builtins.elemAt cols 3;
#       inputAddressed = true;
#     };
#   };
#
# NOTE the fromPath type: builtins.storePath took a STRING and returned a path;
# fetchClosure's fromPath takes the store path as a STRING literal too (the tsv
# column is already "/nix/store/...."), so `builtins.elemAt cols 3` is passed
# unchanged — do NOT wrap it in storePath (that would re-introduce the forbidden
# builtin) and do NOT coerce to a path with ./. .
